/// \defgroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search StringBase character access, prefix and suffix tests, selected comparisons and tokenizing
/// \ingroup grp_common_services
/// \brief StringBase's character access and emptiness tests, its prefix
///        and suffix tests, the single-character compare and compareNoCase
///        overloads, the C-string compareNoCase, reverse search, and the
///        destructive tokenizer nextToken with its four separator-scanning
///        helpers.
///
/// This group does not cover every read-only StringBase member. The other
/// compare and compareNoCase overloads, find, getLength, str, isNone and
/// isNotNone are outside it.
///
/// `StringBase<T>` is the template under both engine string classes:
/// `StringBase<char>` holds an AsciiString's text and
/// `StringBase<unsigned short>` holds a UnicodeString's UTF-16 text (see
/// \ref sub_common_services_strings for the buffer model). Every member in
/// this group exists in both instantiations and is documented once, for a
/// generic `T`. Where the two instantiations work differently, the entry
/// says so.
///
/// \par Shared conventions
/// - **Length, not terminator.** The comparison and search members read
///   the length stored in the string's buffer header, never a terminating
///   NUL, so text with an embedded NUL compares and searches by its full
///   stored length. A string with no buffer behaves as an empty string of
///   length 0. The length counts characters (UTF-16 code units for the
///   wide instantiation), not bytes. nextToken is the exception: its two
///   helpers scan the text up to the terminator.
/// - **Returned pointers are short-lived.** A pointer obtained from str or
///   from reverseFind points into the string's buffer. It is valid only
///   until the string is next changed (nextToken and the setters replace
///   or rewrite the buffer) or destroyed.
/// - **Three overloads per test.** Each prefix or suffix test comes as a
///   length-bounded worker taking `(const T *str, int len)`, an overload
///   taking another StringBase, which passes that string's characters and
///   stored length to the worker, and an overload taking a C string, which
///   measures it with strlen or wcslen first.
/// - **The empty-argument rule.** The prefix and suffix tests return true
///   as soon as the first character of the argument is NUL; they do not
///   look at `len` for that. Zero Hour's AsciiString tests start the same
///   way ("everything starts with the empty string"). Two consequences
///   follow from the bodies: none of these members accepts a null pointer
///   (the C-string overloads measure a null pointer as length 0 but then
///   read its first character anyway), and a worker given an explicit
///   length returns true for any argument whose first character is NUL.
/// - **Comparison results.** The compare and compareNoCase overloads in
///   this group follow the C library's sign convention: negative, zero or
///   positive. The value is either the nonzero result of the character
///   comparison or, when the compared characters match, the difference of
///   the two lengths. Only the sign is meaningful; callers must not rely on
///   the magnitude.
/// - **The wide comparison helper.** Where the narrow members compare with
///   memcmp or _memicmp, the wide members compare through a small
///   out-of-line helper (the reconstruction calls it WideCharCompare;
///   retail exports no name for it) that compares UTF-16 code unit values,
///   or towlower-folded values for the NoCase members.
/// - **Case folding.** The narrow NoCase members fold through the C
///   runtime's _memicmp, byte by byte. The wide ones fold each UTF-16 code
///   unit through the C runtime's towlower, inside the wide comparison
///   helper. How far either folds beyond the ASCII letters depends on the
///   C runtime's locale, which has not been traced for BFME 2.
///
/// \par Where they are used
/// The callers named in this group are examples taken from direct calls in
/// matched BFME 2 code, not complete lists. They include the INI reader
/// (INI::getNextTokenOrNull, INI::prepFile,
/// INI::parseAsciiStringVectorAppend, and parsers such as
/// AudioEventInfo::parseSubsoundsList), map-path handling in GameState and
/// MapCache, command-line handling (GameEngine::init, parseMod), name
/// lookups such as GameLODManager::getStaticGameLODIndex, and the online
/// custom-match screen (AptOnlineCustomMatch::handleSlashCommands).
///
/// \par BFME 2 compared with Zero Hour
/// Zero Hour's AsciiString works on NUL-terminated text: its prefix and
/// suffix tests use strncmp and strnicmp, compareNoCase uses _stricmp,
/// isEmpty tests the first character and reverseFind uses strrchr. The
/// BFME 2 members compare a counted range with memcmp, _memicmp or the wide
/// comparison helper and break ties by length, isEmpty tests the stored
/// length, and reverseFind searches only the stored length. For ordinary
/// text, which has no embedded NUL, the results agree in sign. Zero Hour's
/// UnicodeString has no prefix, suffix or reverse-search members at all;
/// in BFME 2 both instantiations get the full set from the one template.
/// The single-character compare(T) and compareNoCase(T) overloads are not
/// in Zero Hour, and the Open-BFME-1 StringBase that this class was carried
/// from declares neither.
///
/// \par Source layout
/// The retail code shape shows that some StringBase members were built
/// for speed and others for size, and that for some members the char copy
/// and the unsigned short copy were built differently. The ledger
/// (`reverse/functions.csv`) records which reconstruction unit holds each
/// instantiation.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Nothing here reads game data, but these members decide how data text
///   is split and matched. nextToken with no separator list splits on
///   space, line feed, carriage return and tab, and the INI reader passes
///   its own separator set through INI::getNextTokenOrNull.
/// - Lookups that use compareNoCase ignore letter case: the isNone test
///   accepts `None`, `NONE` or `none`, and AudioEventInfo::parseSubsoundsList
///   recognizes `NoSound` in any case. Prefix and suffix tests used for paths
///   and file extensions (for example `.map` and `.rep` in GameEngine::init)
///   come in exact and NoCase forms; which one a caller uses decides
///   whether case matters.
/// - Most game-engine names and paths are AsciiString values and most
///   player-visible text is UnicodeString, so changes here reach the INI
///   reader, map-path handling and UI text alike. Not every engine string
///   is a StringBase: INI::getNextTokenOrNull hands out raw C-string
///   tokens, and W3D/WWLib code also uses WWLib's own StringClass.
/// - Changing the results of these members changes every caller at once,
///   including the INI reader and the code that runs while a map loads.
///   Two builds that split or compare text differently can read the same
///   data differently, which can put a multiplayer game or a replay out of
///   step with the build that made it.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// - Wide strings are compared and searched by UTF-16 code unit, with no
///   awareness of surrogate pairs, combining characters or collation. A
///   port that wants locale-correct ordering or case folding has to replace
///   these members, not just the C runtime calls inside them.
/// - The narrow strings can hold UTF-8 text (AsciiString's translate
///   writes UTF-8), and _memicmp folds bytes, not characters.
/// - String buffers have a size ceiling, and the check is on the size
///   requested, not on the length the text ends up needing.
///   StringBase::ensureUniqueBufferOfSize, which allocates new buffers,
///   throws (an `int` with the value 1) when the requested size (the buffer
///   header plus room for the characters and a terminator) exceeds 32,767
///   bytes.
///   A string set in one step can therefore hold up to roughly 32K narrow
///   characters or 16K UTF-16 code units. Appending reaches the ceiling
///   sooner: when an append outgrows the current buffer, the request is
///   first raised to about 1.5 times the current capacity, so appending to
///   a string whose capacity is above roughly 21.8K narrow characters or
///   10.9K code units throws even when the result would fit. The 16-bit
///   length and capacity fields in the buffer header could not go past
///   65,535 in any case, so a port that needs longer strings has to change
///   both the limit and the header.
/// - The tokenizer helpers are file-static functions that retail calls
///   with a private register convention. That is a compiler artefact, not
///   part of any interface; a port can use any calling convention.

/// \fn T StringBase<T>::getCharAt(int index) const throw()
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns the character at a position, or 0 for an empty string.
///
/// When the string has a buffer, returns the character at `index`
/// directly. When it has none, returns 0 whatever `index` is. There is no
/// bounds check: Zero Hour's AsciiString::getCharAt asserted that the index
/// was inside the string in debug builds, and BFME 2 retail has no such
/// check. Reading at the length returns the terminator, and reading past
/// the capacity reads whatever memory follows the buffer.
///
/// The header declares it non-throwing because retail compiles calls to it
/// that way. Direct callers include LanguageFilter::unHaxor and
/// GameSpyStagingRoom::generateGameSpyGameResultsPacket.
/// \param index Zero-based position. Must be within the string for a
///              meaningful result; it is not checked.
/// \return The character at `index`, or 0 if the string has no buffer.
/// \see StringBase::isEmpty

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::isEmpty() const throw()
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if the string has no buffer or a stored length of
///        0.
///
/// Zero Hour's AsciiString::isEmpty instead tested whether the first
/// character was NUL, so a string whose first character is NUL counts as
/// empty there but not here. The header declares it non-throwing because
/// retail compiles calls to it that way.
///
/// Callers that test UnicodeString text with it include
/// Drawable::setCaptionText, GameState::saveGame,
/// HotKeyManager::searchHotKey, UserPreferences::write and
/// GameSpyLoginPreferences::write; GameEngine::init tests the command-line
/// file name with it.
/// \return true when the string holds no characters.
/// \see StringBase::isNotEmpty, StringBase::getCharAt

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::isNotEmpty() const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns the opposite of isEmpty.
///
/// Calls isEmpty and inverts the result. Zero Hour defines it the same way.
/// \return true when the string holds at least one character.
/// \see StringBase::isEmpty

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::startsWith(const T *str, int len) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string begins with the first `len`
///        characters of `str`, compared exactly.
///
/// The length-bounded worker behind the other two startsWith overloads.
/// It returns true at once if the first character of `str` is NUL, and
/// false if this string is shorter than `len`. Otherwise it compares the
/// first `len` characters of both: the char instantiation with memcmp,
/// the unsigned short one with the wide comparison helper, which compares
/// code unit values.
///
/// Zero Hour's AsciiString::startsWith has no length-bounded form: it
/// measures a C string with strlen and compares with strncmp.
/// PlayerList::validateTeam calls the char worker directly.
/// \param str Characters to look for. Must not be null; see the
///            empty-argument rule in \ref grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search.
/// \param len Number of characters of `str` to compare.
/// \return true if the prefix matches, or if `str` starts with NUL.
/// \see StringBase::startsWithNoCase(const T *str, int len) const,
///      StringBase::endsWith(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::startsWith(const StringBase<T> &str) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string begins with the whole of another
///        string, compared exactly.
///
/// Passes the other string's characters and stored length to the
/// length-bounded worker. An empty `str` is passed as an empty C string, so
/// the result is then always true. The script-engine helpers
/// _appendMessage and _adjustVariable call the char instantiation.
/// \param str The prefix to look for.
/// \return true if this string starts with `str`, or if `str` is empty.
/// \see StringBase::startsWith(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::startsWith(const T *str) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string begins with a NUL-terminated string,
///        compared exactly.
///
/// Measures `str` and runs the length-bounded test. The char copy
/// measures with strlen and calls the worker; the unsigned short copy
/// measures with wcslen and does the comparison itself, in the same way.
/// The `-mod` command-line handler parseMod uses the wide form to detect
/// a path starting with a slash or backslash.
/// \param str The prefix to look for. Must not be null.
/// \return true if this string starts with `str`, or if `str` is empty.
/// \see StringBase::startsWith(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::endsWith(const T *str, int len) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string ends with the first `len`
///        characters of `str`, compared exactly.
///
/// The length-bounded worker behind the other two endsWith overloads. It
/// returns true at once if the first character of `str` is NUL, and false
/// if this string is shorter than `len`. Otherwise it compares the last
/// `len` characters of this string with the first `len` of `str`, through
/// memcmp for char and the wide comparison helper for unsigned short.
///
/// Zero Hour's AsciiString::endsWith has no length-bounded form: it
/// measures a C string with strlen and compares with strncmp.
/// \param str Characters to look for. Must not be null.
/// \param len Number of characters of `str` to compare.
/// \return true if the suffix matches, or if `str` starts with NUL.
/// \see StringBase::endsWithNoCase(const T *str, int len) const,
///      StringBase::startsWith(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::endsWith(const StringBase<T> &str) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string ends with the whole of another
///        string, compared exactly.
///
/// Passes the other string's characters and stored length to the
/// length-bounded worker; an empty `str` always matches.
/// \param str The suffix to look for.
/// \return true if this string ends with `str`, or if `str` is empty.
/// \see StringBase::endsWith(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::endsWith(const T *str) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string ends with a NUL-terminated string,
///        compared exactly.
///
/// Measures `str` with strlen or wcslen and calls the length-bounded
/// worker. parseMod uses the wide form to see whether a mod path already
/// ends with a slash or backslash before it appends one.
/// \param str The suffix to look for. Must not be null.
/// \return true if this string ends with `str`, or if `str` is empty.
/// \see StringBase::endsWith(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::startsWithNoCase(const T *str, int len) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string begins with the first `len`
///        characters of `str`, ignoring letter case.
///
/// The same steps as the exact worker: true if the first character of
/// `str` is NUL, false if this string is shorter than `len`, and otherwise
/// a comparison of the first `len` characters, through _memicmp for char
/// and the wide comparison helper (towlower on each code unit) for
/// unsigned short.
/// \param str Characters to look for. Must not be null.
/// \param len Number of characters of `str` to compare.
/// \return true if the prefix matches without regard to case, or if `str`
///         starts with NUL.
/// \see StringBase::startsWith(const T *str, int len) const,
///      StringBase::endsWithNoCase(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::startsWithNoCase(const StringBase<T> &str) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string begins with the whole of another
///        string, ignoring letter case.
///
/// Passes the other string's characters and stored length to the
/// length-bounded worker; an empty `str` always matches.
/// GameState::realMapPathToPortableMapPath calls the char instantiation to
/// test a map path against each of several directory prefixes in turn.
/// \param str The prefix to look for.
/// \return true if this string starts with `str` without regard to case,
///         or if `str` is empty.
/// \see StringBase::startsWithNoCase(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::startsWithNoCase(const T *str) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string begins with a NUL-terminated string,
///        ignoring letter case.
///
/// Measures `str` and runs the length-bounded test. The char copy measures
/// with an inlined strlen and does the _memicmp comparison itself; the
/// unsigned short copy measures with wcslen and calls the worker.
/// GameState's map-path conversions (GameState::unpackPortableMapPath,
/// GameState::portableMapPathToRealMapPath), MapCache::writeCacheINI and
/// MapCache::clearUnseenMaps call the char form.
/// \param str The prefix to look for. Must not be null.
/// \return true if this string starts with `str` without regard to case,
///         or if `str` is empty.
/// \see StringBase::startsWithNoCase(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::endsWithNoCase(const T *str, int len) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string ends with the first `len` characters
///        of `str`, ignoring letter case.
///
/// The same steps as the exact suffix worker, comparing the last `len`
/// characters through _memicmp for char and the wide comparison helper's
/// case-folding comparison for unsigned short.
/// \param str Characters to look for. Must not be null.
/// \param len Number of characters of `str` to compare.
/// \return true if the suffix matches without regard to case, or if `str`
///         starts with NUL.
/// \see StringBase::endsWith(const T *str, int len) const,
///      StringBase::startsWithNoCase(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::endsWithNoCase(const StringBase<T> &str) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string ends with the whole of another
///        string, ignoring letter case.
///
/// Passes the other string's characters and stored length to the
/// length-bounded worker; an empty `str` always matches.
/// \param str The suffix to look for.
/// \return true if this string ends with `str` without regard to case, or
///         if `str` is empty.
/// \see StringBase::endsWithNoCase(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::endsWithNoCase(const T *str) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns true if this string ends with a NUL-terminated string,
///        ignoring letter case; the usual file-extension test.
///
/// Measures `str` with strlen or wcslen and calls the length-bounded
/// worker. Callers of the char form include GameEngine::init, which tells a
/// map (`.map`) from a replay (`.rep`) named on the command line,
/// MapCache::loadUserMaps, Win32Mouse::initCursorResources and
/// ConvertShortMapPathToLongMapPath. GameStateMap::clearScratchPadMaps uses
/// the wide form.
/// \param str The suffix to look for. Must not be null.
/// \return true if this string ends with `str` without regard to case, or
///         if `str` is empty.
/// \see StringBase::endsWithNoCase(const T *str, int len) const

/// \fn int StringBase<T>::compare(T c) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Compares this string with a one-character string made of `c`.
///
/// Compares the first character (none if the string is empty) with `c`;
/// if they differ, the result is the character comparison's nonzero
/// result (only its sign is meaningful), and otherwise it is this string's
/// length minus 1. The result is therefore 0 only when the string is
/// exactly `c`, and an empty string compares below any character. The char
/// copy compares with memcmp, so the bytes compare as unsigned values; the
/// unsigned short copy uses the shared wide range comparison with the wide
/// comparison helper.
///
/// Not in Zero Hour or in the Open-BFME-1 StringBase; retail exports both
/// instantiations.
/// \param c The character to compare against.
/// \return Negative, zero or positive, with the sign meaning of memcmp.
/// \see StringBase::compareNoCase(T c) const

/// \fn int StringBase<T>::compareNoCase(T c) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Compares this string with a one-character string made of `c`,
///        ignoring letter case.
///
/// Works like compare(T): the first character is compared with `c`, here
/// through _memicmp for char and through the wide comparison helper
/// (towlower on both code units) for unsigned short, and a match falls
/// back to this string's length minus 1.
/// \param c The character to compare against.
/// \return Negative, zero or positive; 0 only when the string is exactly
///         `c` apart from letter case.
/// \see StringBase::compare(T c) const

/// \fn int StringBase<T>::compareNoCase(const T *str) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Compares this string with a NUL-terminated string, ignoring
///        letter case.
///
/// Measures `str` (0 when it is null), compares the shorter of the two
/// lengths with _memicmp for char or the wide comparison helper's
/// case-folding comparison for unsigned short, and when those characters match
/// returns this string's length minus the length of `str`. Zero Hour's
/// AsciiString::compareNoCase used _stricmp on the terminated text instead;
/// the sign agrees for ordinary text.
///
/// This is the engine's usual case-insensitive name test. isNone and
/// isNotNone compare against the word None with it. Callers include the
/// INI parsers AudioEventInfo::parseSubsoundsList (which recognizes the
/// name NoSound this way) and Eva::iniParseNewEvaEvent, and name lookups
/// such as GameLODManager::getStaticGameLODIndex.
/// ChatWindowsOnlineBuddy::PopulatePlayerList uses the wide form.
/// \param str The string to compare with. A null pointer is measured as
///            length 0, so the character comparison covers no characters
///            and the result is this string's length, as for an empty
///            string.
/// \return Negative, zero or positive; 0 when the two strings have the
///         same length and match apart from letter case.
/// \see StringBase::compareNoCase(T c) const

/// \fn const T * StringBase<T>::reverseFind(T c) const
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Returns a pointer to the last occurrence of `c` in the string,
///        or null.
///
/// Walks back from the end of the stored length to the first character.
/// Unlike Zero Hour's AsciiString::reverseFind, which used strrchr, it
/// never finds the terminator: searching for NUL returns null unless the
/// text holds an embedded NUL.
///
/// Paths are its main use. GameState::getMapLeafName and
/// GetBasePathFromPath find the last backslash with it, and
/// MapCache::addMap, MapCache::loadUserMaps and
/// StatsCollector::createFileName also call it.
/// \param c The character to find.
/// \return A pointer into this string's buffer, valid until the string is
///         changed or destroyed, or null if `c` does not occur.
/// \see StringBase::nextToken, \ref grp_w2_map_file_paths

/// \fn bool StringBase<T>::nextToken(StringBase<T> *out, const T *delimiters)
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief Removes the next separator-delimited token from the front of
///        this string and stores it in `out`.
///
/// Works like strtok, but on strings rather than a shared static pointer:
/// - If this string is empty, or `out` is this same string, it returns
///   false and changes neither string.
/// - With no separator list it uses space, line feed, carriage return and
///   tab.
/// - It skips leading separators (skipSeps), then finds the end of the
///   token (skipNonSeps). If there is a token, `out` receives a copy of it,
///   NUL-terminated, and this string keeps everything from the end of the
///   token onwards, including the separator that ended it; it returns true.
/// - If only separators were left, both strings are emptied and it returns
///   false.
///
/// Repeated calls therefore consume a string token by token. Zero Hour's
/// AsciiString::nextToken behaves the same way for ordinary text; it
/// measured the remainder up to its terminator, where BFME 2 passes the
/// remaining stored length. The unsigned short copy
/// (with skipSepsW and skipNonSepsW) is the same algorithm.
///
/// Callers of the char form include INI::getNextTokenOrNull, which takes
/// tokens from the INI reader's pending text through it and falls back to
/// strtok once that text is used up, INI::prepFile, INI::parseAsciiStringVectorAppend,
/// PlayerList::newGame, SidesList::validateAllyEnemyList,
/// UserPreferences::load, GameInfo::setMap and
/// AptOnlineCustomMatch::handleSlashCommands. Callers of the wide form
/// include LanguageFilter::filterLine, the SkirmishPreferences constructor
/// and Win32LocalFileSystem::openFile.
/// \param out [out] Receives the token. Must not be null or this string.
///            Emptied when no token is found, left alone when this string
///            was already empty.
/// \param delimiters Separator characters as a NUL-terminated list, or
///                   null for the default set.
/// \return true if a token was found and stored in `out`.
/// \par Modding
/// The default separator set is fixed in the body. Text split with it
/// cannot contain a token with an embedded space; callers that need one
/// pass their own separators.

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/WideStrLenSource.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief StringBase<char>::nextToken and the two file-static helpers that
///        scan for separators, skipSeps and skipNonSeps.

/// \fn static char * skipSeps(char *p, const char *seps)
/// \brief Advances past every leading character that is in the separator
///        set.
///
/// Returns the first character of `p` that is not in `seps`, or the
/// terminating NUL. With an empty separator set nothing is skipped and `p`
/// is returned unchanged. The scan relies on NUL termination, not on a
/// stored length. Zero Hour's AsciiString.cpp has a helper of the same name
/// that StringBase::nextToken's Zero Hour twin uses for the same purpose.
/// \param p Start of the text; must be NUL-terminated.
/// \param seps NUL-terminated list of separator characters.
/// \return Pointer to the first non-separator character, or to the NUL.
/// \see skipNonSeps, StringBase::nextToken

/// \fn static char * skipNonSeps(char *p, const char *seps)
/// \brief Advances to the first character that is in the separator set.
///
/// Returns the first character of `p` that is in `seps`, or the
/// terminating NUL. With an empty separator set it runs to the end of the
/// text. StringBase::nextToken calls it right after skipSeps, so the
/// distance between the two results is the token's length.
/// \param p Start of the token; must be NUL-terminated.
/// \param seps NUL-terminated list of separator characters.
/// \return Pointer to the first separator after the token, or to the NUL.
/// \see skipSeps, StringBase::nextToken

/// \file WWVegas/WWLib/StringBaseSkipSepsW.cpp
/// \ingroup grp_w1021_stringbase_compare_search
/// \brief StringBase<unsigned short>::nextToken and the two file-static
///        helpers that scan UTF-16 text for separators, skipSepsW and
///        skipNonSepsW.

/// \fn static unsigned short * skipSepsW(unsigned short *p, const unsigned short *seps)
/// \brief UTF-16 version of skipSeps: advances past leading separator code
///        units.
///
/// Same behaviour as skipSeps, one 16-bit code unit at a time. A separator
/// set can only name single code units, so characters outside the Basic
/// Multilingual Plane cannot be separators.
/// \param p Start of the text; must be NUL-terminated.
/// \param seps NUL-terminated list of separator code units.
/// \return Pointer to the first non-separator code unit, or to the NUL.
/// \see skipNonSepsW, skipSeps

/// \fn static unsigned short * skipNonSepsW(unsigned short *p, const unsigned short *seps)
/// \brief UTF-16 version of skipNonSeps: advances to the first separator
///        code unit.
///
/// Same behaviour as skipNonSeps, one 16-bit code unit at a time.
/// \param p Start of the token; must be NUL-terminated.
/// \param seps NUL-terminated list of separator code units.
/// \return Pointer to the first separator after the token, or to the NUL.
/// \see skipSepsW, skipNonSeps
